# TypeScript and React Native Standards

- Use strict, explicit types for props, params, and function returns on public helpers.
- Avoid `any`; if unavoidable, isolate and document why it is needed.
- Prefer named exports in shared modules unless file already follows default-export style.
- Keep components small and focused; move repeated logic into hooks or utilities.

## React Native specifics

- Prefer platform-safe styles and primitives already used in the codebase.
- Keep UI text and constants centralized instead of hardcoding repeated strings.
- Handle loading, empty, and error states explicitly in screen-level components.
